--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -193,3 +193,7 @@
 Schema version 4 adds portable `budget` and `activities` questions. Their configs hold frozen bilingual catalogs, optional external references and calculation settings. Answers are version-1 JSON strings containing stable rows. `grantIssues`, `calculateBudget`, `normalizeBudgetAnswer`, field descriptors and the optional Vue `useGrantElement` support host-controlled rendering and validation. Version 3 remains strict. No database transfer or themed renderer is supplied. Budgets preserve exact decimal money, signed cost/program adjustments, nonnegative distinct outside funding allocations and year/currency percentage calculations rounded to whole dollars. Activities preserve bilingual text, calendar dates, outcomes and responsible-party selections. Hosts must validate incomplete draft catalogs at publication and resolve external identities before transferring data.
 
 Version 4 also provides `checkboxes` and `multiselect` questions with bilingual options. Answers are JSON arrays of unique allowed option IDs; required means at least one selection on submit. Multiple-choice conditions support exact `contains` membership and answered/unanswered. Ordinary v4 table questions can specify `totals: 'none' | 'rows' | 'columns' | 'both'`; `tableTotals` derives exact decimal row/column sums from numeric cells, returning null for totals affected by invalid nonempty cells. Totals are display-only and do not add answer keys.
+
+## Currency-aware grant budgets
+
+Grant form drafts keep human major-unit decimal strings. Every monetary validation, calculation and aggregate uses the public `@gcs-ssc/extensions/money` implementation backed by Dinero and the row's actual currency. Fractional minor units are rejected; JPY whole units, KWD thousandths, CLF ten-thousandths and MGA fifths are supported. The currency catalog is the SDK's supported settlement catalog, with no guessed two-decimal fallback. Percentage charges preserve the explicit whole-major-unit half-away-from-zero rounding rule. Normalized human decimal strings omit insignificant trailing zeros. The former `moneyCents` and `centsText` exports are removed.
